ess you  so i've been a whole term firefighter  for just over four years  so what's changed for you in the last  few months then it's difficult pretty  much like you you're kind of having an  overlook on everything now it's you're  kind of going back to basics and  thinking do i need this anymore so when  it gets to a point where you can't cover  the bills because  you're not hit inflation's that high and  you're not even keeping up anywhere near  with inflation  i feel like something has to be done  i'd like to have enough money to not  have to worry about things  the government says a hike in public  sector pay would cause a spike in demand  for goods and services and that would  cause prices to rise even more we can't  fix the increase in the cost of living  just by increasing wages to match the  surge in prices  but the cost of living isn't slowing  down either new forecasts suggest energy  bills could rise to more than 4  200 pounds in april wiping out the 15  billion pound support package the  government has pledged to help  struggling families  where are my school shoes well you need  your trainers don't you the other thing  is my school shoes are your trainers  i've come to meet rachel badzier a  special needs teacher and her two  children violet age 13 and billy age 10.  how difficult is it to try to keep  trying to find those savings and trying  to balance the defense well it is i just  have to make sure that i don't overspend  but you notice that where i could spend  30 40 quid i'm now spending 60 pounds  because and i don't really think that  i've bought anything different  rachel says her salary of 35 000 pounds  a year isn't going up enough to match  the rising cost of living  pay rises for public sector workers like  nurses and teachers are determined by  independent pay review bodies  the pay body sh will be looking at  nurses railway workers  and teachers and there will have to be  there will have to be priorities made so  i mean i mean and i can understand it's  not fine we don't there's no finite  purse that i mean there never has been  and so it is that thing of where you've  got to think  is it how can you prioritize yes you  might be a nurse and you save lives in  that way however in the flip side of  that the reason that you are a nurse is  because you have teachers to help you  get through your life to get to where  you are  more and more people are saying actually  enough is enough we've got no money it's  coming to be like a big 70s britain  by the late 1970s britain led by  labour's james callahan was in serious  financial trouble  and i emphasize to you that if we fail  to bring down inflation we shall never  succeed in overcoming unemployment  millions of working days were being lost  to the strikes and in the end workers  secured the wage rise as they were  fighting for  but it left labor damaged  and they lost the next election  katie has been offered a 4 pay rise this  year do you see strikes coming down the  line in the nursing profession yes  absolutely i want to say that we just  walk out and you but then you know who  does look after the patients appears to  me that you're not ready to strike  at the moment i'm not but that's at the  moment and if this continues then i will  take some kind of industrial action but  i prefer it not to be needed i prefer  the government just listen to us do you  think they will  i don't know  i don't think so  ready one two three go go go  firefighters like adam have been offered  a 2 pay rise this year the fire brigades  union called that offer utterly  inadequate good vlad high five high five  good man  if the offer is not increased in the  autumn firefighters will be asked to  vote on a strike  if it came to it would you strike  it's a it's a very difficult question  because  no no firefighter wants to strike a lot  of us live in that community so it has  also an effect on ourselves as well not  just everyone else and and we do the job  because we want to help people we do  need to listen to it and there is a lot  of firefighters that are struggling with  the wages and just trying to pay the  bills never mind having any sort of  luxuries in with anything  today we're going to learn all about  careers  rachel's part of the national education  union this autumn members will be asked  if they're prepared to strike  the union is calling it the biggest  ballot of teachers in a generation  are you worried that the public  parents in particular i think  might not have as much sympathy with  teachers who want to strike as perhaps  you would like definitely i can i can  definitely understand that because being  a parent myself you know the impact that  teachers not being in school has on your  own children and you know the disruption  that it can cause and i do think that  that adds an extra weight to my decision  making  so you're not decided i'm not fully  decided  that's my dad  he was in the territorial army so i've  got his uh medals as well  for the first time since she qualified  as a nurse katie is considering her  future  how would it feel  if it came to the  crunch and you had to give it up  i don't want to think about that  i remember the look on my dad's face  before he passed away  and i told him that i'd finally got the  job and  he said good on you kid  i love my job  i worked so hard for it and i made my  dad so proud by getting it  i don't want to give this up  i can't give it up not yet  that's why i'm fighting now  well it's right that we reward our  hard-working public sector workers with  a pay rise but that needs to be  proportionate and balanced with the need  not to make the inflationary pressures  worse and also to see what's affordable  for the taxpayer i'm on the side of  people who work hard who go into work  who want to run their businesses we  can't see them hampered by the  activities of these militant unions  the next few weeks and months could see  millions of workers balloted for strike  actio